{"name":"Honey Fungus","hex":"#d18e54","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"honey fungus","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A parasitic mushroom of the genus Armillaria that lives on trees and shrubs."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/honey_fungus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Armillaria","blocks":[{"text":"Armillaria is a genus of fungi that includes the A. mellea species that live on trees and woody shrubs. It includes about 10 species formerly categorized summarily as A. mellea. Armillaria sp. are long-lived and form the largest living fungi in the world. The largest known specimen covers more than 3.4 square miles (8.8 km2) in Oregon and is estimated to be 2,500 years old. Some species of Armillaria display bioluminescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Armillaria_mellea%2C_Honey_Fungus%2C_UK_1.jpg/330px-Armillaria_mellea%2C_Honey_Fungus%2C_UK_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armillaria","wikidata":"Q952036"},{"title":"Honey","blocks":[{"text":"Honey is a sweet and viscous substance made by several species of bees, the best-known of which are honey bees. Honey is made and stored to nourish bee colonies. Bees produce honey by gathering and then refining the sugary secretions of plants or the secretions of other insects, like the honeydew of aphids. This refinement takes place both within individual bees, through regurgitation and enzymatic activity, and during storage in the hive, through water evaporation that concentrates the honey's sugars until it is thick and viscous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Runny_hunny.jpg/330px-Runny_hunny.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honey","wikidata":"Q10987"},{"title":"Fungus","blocks":[{"text":"A fungus is any member of the group of eukaryotic organisms that includes yeasts, molds, and mushrooms. These organisms are classified in the biological kingdom Fungi."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Fungi_collage.jpg/330px-Fungi_collage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fungus","wikidata":"Q764"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Armillaria_mellea_041031w.jpg/500px-Armillaria_mellea_041031w.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Armillaria_mellea_041031w.jpg/1280px-Armillaria_mellea_041031w.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Armillaria_mellea_041031w.jpg/500px-Armillaria_mellea_041031w.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Armillaria mellea","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArmillaria_mellea_041031w.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Armillaria_sp_Marriott_edit.jpg/500px-Armillaria_sp_Marriott_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Armillaria_sp_Marriott_edit.jpg/1280px-Armillaria_sp_Marriott_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Armillaria_sp_Marriott_edit.jpg/500px-Armillaria_sp_Marriott_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Armillaria hinnulea","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArmillaria_sp_Marriott_edit.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Mycelial_cord_Armillaria_%28rhizomorphs%29.jpg/500px-Mycelial_cord_Armillaria_%28rhizomorphs%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_ca